<decision #track.adopt-halfcode-system-skill-distribution.authority-boundary {
  priority = "P0"
  status = "accepted"
  durable_candidate = true
} (
  <question ?>Which system owns generated Skill dependency and which system owns the global installation transaction?</?>
  <answer (
    <raw-answer ?>Halfcode owns distribution semantics; Eidolon owns the host tree transaction.</?>
    <decision-text ?>Eidolon SHALL invoke the Halfcode plan/applier in isolation and SHALL NOT reproduce capsule dependency, topology, collision or content validation. Eidolon SHALL own only global path binding, ordinary asset preservation, unified manifest and whole-root replacement.</?>
    <rationale ?>This keeps generic resource standards in Halfcode while preserving host-specific filesystem policy in Eidolon.</?>
    <evidence ?>Halfcode 0.2.2 exposes an immutable Resource DSL Skill plan and atomic output-root applier; the current Eidolon installer swaps each Skill separately.</?>
  )>
  <confidence ?>0.99</?>
  <reversibility ?>low</?>
)>

<decision #track.adopt-halfcode-system-skill-distribution.transaction-scope {
  priority = "P0"
  status = "accepted"
  durable_candidate = true
} (
  <question ?>What is the atomic visibility boundary for global init?</?>
  <answer (
    <raw-answer ?>The entire global `skills` root.</?>
    <decision-text ?>The installer SHALL build and verify a same-parent complete candidate, then replace the live `skills` root once with recoverable backup handling.</?>
    <rationale ?>Per-Skill replacement permits a mixed managed set and separates the manifest from the installed tree.</?>
    <evidence ?>Current production loops over managed Skills and swaps each directory before writing `.system-skills.xnl`.</?>
  )>
  <confidence ?>0.99</?>
  <reversibility ?>moderate</?>
)>

<decision #track.adopt-halfcode-system-skill-distribution.transition-scope {
  priority = "P1"
  status = "accepted"
} (
  <question ?>Does this infrastructure track remove the legacy `sys-ai-workflow` identity?</?>
  <answer (
    <raw-answer ?>No; it remains transitional until the dedicated split track.</?>
    <decision-text ?>This track SHALL install `sys-ai-workflow@1.0.7` and generated `sys-halfcode-resource-dsl@1.0.0`; the next track owns the three Eidolon Anchor Skill identities and old identity retirement.</?>
    <rationale ?>Separating transaction infrastructure from content migration keeps rollback and compatibility independently verifiable.</?>
    <evidence ?>The active mission already has a following `split-eidolon-anchor-devops-authoring-and-run-skills` node.</?>
  )>
  <confidence ?>0.98</?>
  <reversibility ?>high</?>
)>

<decision #track.adopt-halfcode-system-skill-distribution.read-authority {
  priority = "P0"
  status = "accepted"
  durable_candidate = true
} (
  <question ?>How does runtime read an installed generated system Skill without a hardcoded generated inventory?</?>
  <answer (
    <raw-answer ?>Through exact entries and file digests in the unified installed manifest.</?>
    <decision-text ?>Generic system Skill resource reads SHALL require an exact `.system-skills.xnl` identity/path entry and verify the file digest; product wrappers may select explicit paths but cannot infer semantic routing.</?>
    <rationale ?>The installed manifest is the host readback of current plans and avoids duplicating Halfcode identity constants in runtime code.</?>
    <evidence ?>The existing reader only validates known bundled Skills and otherwise reads arbitrary relative paths without a manifest digest check.</?>
  )>
  <confidence ?>0.97</?>
  <reversibility ?>moderate</?>
)>
